{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And now come please, etc.</b> He said <b>and now</b> — that he should not delay even one hour, for every hour they are in danger from their side. And his saying <b>please</b> is an expression of request, and he attached the request to the essence of the action, which is ״אָרָה לִי״ (curse for me) etc., and he was precise in saying the word <b>for me</b> — that the cursing upon them should be for me, for my need. And the intention in this is that the cursing should not be delayed until after they have taken vengeance upon them, and what was — was; rather, that the action be done immediately, for in this would be the salvation of Moab from them. And he further hinted in the saying <b>for me</b>: because Balaam had preceded and blessed Balak, as they said, of blessed memory (Tanhuma, Balak 1) — therefore he said ״אָרָה לִי״, meaning: even for me, who is already blessed, this will not avail, and you must curse them. And if you would say: he has no need, for you are already blessed — therefore he said <b>for he is mightier than I</b>. Here he included two matters: one, the matter of natural strength; and one, the matter of blessing — for this people's blessing is mightier than my blessing. And his saying <b>perhaps we will strike him</b> means: and after all this, I am still in the category of doubt — perhaps; and even this category of doubt is not to destroy him but to strike him, to drive him away from upon me. Also, he intended to say: concerning the intermediate ones — <b>we will strike him</b>; and concerning the righteous ones — <b>and I will drive him out.</b>" "<b>And by way of</b> allusion, his mouth caused him to stumble in the saying <b>curse for me</b> — to curse Balak. And so it was, that he caused him, through his advice, the death of Cozbi his daughter. It was also the cause that Balak would return to Midian, and there he fell by the sword, as it is written (Numbers 31:8) ״וְאֶת מַלְכֵי מִדְיָן הָרְגוּ״ etc. ״וְאֶת צוּר״ etc., and had it not been for Balaam's going — who revealed to Moab that they are assuredly safe from Israel and that nothing would be done to them until the end of days — Balak would have remained there and would not have returned to Midian and would not have been killed. Behold, it was fulfilled that he went to curse him, to Balak, to fulfill what is said (Psalms 37:15) ״חַרְבָּם תָּבוֹא בְלִבָּם״." "<b>For I know et, etc.</b> It will be explained according to their words, of blessed memory (Numbers Rabbah here), who said that Balaam blessed Balak that he should be king. Therefore he said <b>for I know et whom you bless is blessed</b> — for his blessing has already been fulfilled in him, and this matter is known only to him alone, because he himself tested the matter, and it is not known to all that Balaam's blessing is a [genuine] blessing. And <b>whom you curse is cursed</b> — this is known from the fact that he cursed Moab for Sihon. And one may raise a point regarding this matter: granted, the fact that his curses were accurate has two reasons — one, that he was evil-eyed, and the second, that he knew the moment of wrath, as they said, of blessed memory (Berakhot 7a), and he would curse at the moment of anger; but the blessing — there is no existence that it would be fulfilled from his mouth." "<b>And it appears</b> that Balaam's blessing was always like the blessing of the donkey, except that the wicked man would practice deception: when he would see through astrology that so-and-so would rise to greatness and the like, he would make as though he were blessing him; and when the matter came to pass and was fulfilled, [that person] would think that Balaam's blessing had caused it — but it was not so; rather, his mazzal (destiny) caused it. And so he did to Balak, for he saw in his star that he was destined to reign over Moab, and he blessed him with the very same matter to deceive him, for he was the one who ensnared."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {0 "The verb אָרָה ('curse') is rendered literally throughout. The Aramaic/Hebrew root consistently means 'to curse' in this context.", 2 "The word אֵת in the lemma is transliterated as 'et' per the convention for this particle under discussion; in context it functions as the definite direct object marker before אֲשֶׁר."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:12:16.463420310Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}